# Six-eight time: six quavers to a bar felt as two big beats of three, read in quavers, told from
# three-four by where the notes start, and tapped, with Row, Row, Row Your Boat (traditional) and a
# jig on the midnight sun's ice shelf.
lesson music-six-eight-time v=1 format=teach grade=6 unit=2 subject=music levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Six-eight time"
  goal "Count six-eight as two big beats of three quavers, and read and tap its rhythms."

  look {
    scene 35x13 {
      row top space=2 align=end at=canvas(1, 1) {
        notes row clef=treble notes=[C5, C5, C5, D5, E5] values=[1.5, 1.5, 1, 0.5, 1.5] meter=6 quavers=true
        seal seal rock=1 facing=-1
      }
    }
    say """
      The crew row out from the research hut to count seals, singing Row, Row, Row Your Boat to keep
      their oars together. The song is in six-eight: six quavers in a bar, felt as two big beats of
      three. Count 1 2 3 4 5 6 and pull on 1 and 4. A dotted crotchet lasts three quavers, a whole big
      beat, so each row is one stroke. A crotchet then a quaver fills a big beat too, long then short,
      which is the swing of a jig.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice music.six8-quavers-in count=1 seed=7 easy-seed=5 hard-seed=7
    level easy {
      worked music.six8-where-starts k=2 level=medium
    }
    practice music.six8-where-starts count=1 seed=5 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=7
    practice music.six8-fill-bar count=1 seed=2 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=2
    show music.six8-where-starts k=1 hard-k=4
    show music.six8-tap-row
    practice music.six8-which-jig count=1 seed=2 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
    show music.six8-tap-sledge
    level hard {
      show music.six8-claim q=1 r=1
    }
    practice music.iceberg-row-strokes count=1 seed=2 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show music.six8-claim q=0 r=0 easy-r=1 hard-r=0
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show music.six8-all-ways v=1 easy-v=0 hard-v=2
  }
  remember {
    say "Six-eight is six quavers in a bar, felt as two big beats: 1 2 3, 4 5 6."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is that six-eight counts quavers and groups them in threes, so a bar has two big beats,
    on 1 and 4, and a dotted crotchet lasts one big beat. The wrong ideas to watch for are counting
    six-eight in crotchets, so a dotted crotchet reads as one and a half and the bar never adds up,
    and hearing it as six beats or as three-four, which also holds six quavers but in three twos. In
    the working, look for the child writing the count under each note before answering. The two-star
    question sets a bar of three-four beside a bar of six-eight; the three-star question counts every
    bar made from shapes that each fill a big beat, which is a multiplication once the shapes for
    one beat are listed. A child who is stuck can march or row to the song, stepping on 1 and 4, and
    go back to the way in; stay on this lesson another day if dotted notes are still read short. The
    taps are judged on the music page; on paper you are the judge. Off the page, sing Row, Row, Row
    Your Boat or Here We Go Round the Mulberry Bush and clap only the big beats.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the notes to count are a quaver, crotchet or dotted crotchet, where a note starts is worked once in the teacher's pen before bars of crotchets and quavers, the bars to fill need a quaver, a crotchet or a dotted crotchet, the jig is found among plain waltzes, the crew's strokes are counted for one time through, the two-star is as written with its answers in another order, and the three-star counts bars from two shapes."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the notes to count include the dotted minim, the notes to place come later in bars that start with a quaver or a dotted crotchet, the jig hides among waltzes with long notes across the beat, a claim that six-eight has six beats is judged before the two-star, the crew's distance is turned into times through the song, and the three-star counts bars from four shapes with the two beats different."
  }
}
